# Primitive types, ranges, literals, and overflow

## The eight primitive types
byte, short, int, and long are signed integers with 8, 16, 32, and 64 bits respectively. byte ranges from -128 to 127; int from -2^31 to 2^31-1. char is an unsigned 16-bit UTF-16 code unit. float and double represent binary floating-point approximations. boolean is true or false; Java does not specify a source-level storage size for it.

```java
int population = 2_000_000;
long large = 3_000_000_000L;
double price = 0.1;
char initial = 'J';
boolean active = true;
System.out.println(Integer.MAX_VALUE + 1); // -2147483648
```
The L suffix makes the large literal a long. Single quotes denote a char; double quotes denote a String, which is a reference type. Underscores improve numeric readability without changing the value. Hexadecimal 0x10 and binary 0b10000 both equal decimal 16.

## Overflow is not automatic validation
Ordinary integer arithmetic wraps when the mathematical result exceeds the range. It does not automatically throw. Math.addExact and Math.multiplyExact throw ArithmeticException instead, which is useful when silent wrapping would corrupt a result. Division truncates toward zero: -7 / 2 is -3. Integer division by zero throws; floating-point arithmetic has infinity and NaN.

## Practice
Calculate the number of seconds in 100 years using int and long intermediates. Explain why assigning an already-overflowed int expression to long cannot repair it. Choose types for an age, a database identifier, a flag, and an exact monetary amount, and state the range each application accepts.

# Variables, final, scope, and definite assignment

## Declaration and assignment
A declaration introduces a variable's name and type; assignment supplies its value. A local variable must be definitely assigned along every execution path before it is read. Object fields have default values, but that does not make defaults valid business state.
```java
int score;
boolean passed = true;
if (passed) {
    score = 80;
} else {
    score = 0;
}
System.out.println(score);
final int passingScore = 60;
```
Removing the else leaves a path on which score is unassigned. The compiler rejects the read even if you believe that path will not occur. A variable declared inside braces is visible only within its scope. Prefer the narrowest useful scope and meaningful names such as passingScore rather than p.

## final and var
final prevents assigning a new value to that variable after initialization. For a reference, it does not prevent operations that mutate the referenced object. For example, a final array variable may still have its elements changed. Later immutability lessons address the object itself.

Local var asks the compiler to infer a fixed type from the initializer. `var count = 1;` remains an int variable; assigning text later is illegal. var needs an initializer and cannot infer a type from null alone. Use an explicit type when inference would hide important meaning.

## Practice
Write a three-branch grade classification in which a result is assigned exactly once on every path. Try reading a loop-local variable after the loop. Explain each diagnostic. Then rename ambiguous variables and compare how much explanation the code still needs.

# Console input, parsing, validation, and formatted output

## A line is text until validated
Use Scanner for small console programs, but choose one input style consistently. nextInt consumes a token and can leave its following newline; a subsequent nextLine may return the empty remainder. Reading a whole line and parsing it makes the boundary explicit.
```java
var scanner = new java.util.Scanner(System.in);
System.out.print("Age: ");
String line = scanner.nextLine();
try {
    int age = Integer.parseInt(line.trim());
    if (age < 0 || age > 130) {
        System.out.println("Age must be from 0 to 130");
    } else {
        System.out.printf(java.util.Locale.ROOT, "Age=%d%n", age);
    }
} catch (NumberFormatException error) {
    System.out.println("Enter a whole number");
}
```
This is a method-body snippet. Parsing verifies numeric spelling and range; the following condition verifies the domain range. Neither check replaces the other. Later loop lessons show how to ask again after invalid input.

## Output contracts
%d formats an integer, %.2f formats a floating value with two fractional digits, %s formats text, and %n emits the platform line separator. Locale affects decimal punctuation. Exact machine-readable output should select its locale or use an explicit serialization format.

Closing a Scanner closes its underlying stream. Do not close a shared System.in that another component still needs; assign stream ownership to the application.

## Practice
Try blank input, spaces, -1, 131, 25, and a number too large for int. Record a different diagnosis for malformed text and valid-but-out-of-range numbers. Test formatted output under a locale that uses a decimal comma.

